WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:08.720
In almost every corner of the world human&nbsp;
sacrifices, mostly based on religion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.560
did happen at some point in history. The&nbsp;
most treasured thing a human can give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:18.080
up is his life and, therefore, in the eyes&nbsp;
of many throughout history human sacrifice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:23.680
is the most valuable sacrifice someone can&nbsp;
offer to a god or gods. However, despite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.320
fact that human sacrifices did happen at some&nbsp;
point throughout history all across the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:34.160
no culture brought the act of human sacrifices&nbsp;
to such a large extent as the Aztecs did.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:40.160
The Aztecs were a pre-Columbian civilization&nbsp;
living in today's central Mexico. The word Aztec&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.920
is misleading as it can refer to multiplicities of&nbsp;
peoples and polities but when people use it today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:51.760
they mostly just mean the Mexica people whose&nbsp;
capital was the city of Tenochtitlan. The Mexica,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:57.840
by the time of the early 16th century, when Cortes&nbsp;
arrived to Mexico, where the hegemon of a triple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:04.240
alliance that ruled over a large polity we today&nbsp;
call the Aztec Empire. Therefore, we often use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:10.320
the words Aztec and Mexica as interchangeable&nbsp;
even though they’re really not. The word Aztec&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.440
can refer to many different people groups like,&nbsp;
for example, everyone within the Aztec empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:20.560
or everyone who spoke the Nahuatl language, or&nbsp;
everyone pertaining to the tribes that migrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.920
from the north to central Mexico of whom the&nbsp;
Mexica people were just one tribe. Due to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.880
ambiguous definition of the word Aztec, it&nbsp;
is important to more specifically define&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:36.160
the words that I will use in this video. I will&nbsp;
use the word Mexica to refer to the people from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:42.960
Tenochtitlan who ruled over what we call the Aztec&nbsp;
Empire. I will use the word Aztec as a catch all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:48.080
word for the religion, because that’s what we&nbsp;
call it today, the “Aztec Religion”, and also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:54.400
I will use the word to refer to the polity of the&nbsp;
Aztec Empire as a whole and the people within it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:59.920
even though keep in mind some of these people&nbsp;
were not always happy to be in the empire and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:06.320
they often saw themselves as different from the&nbsp;
ruling Mexica. Lastly I’ll use the word Nahua to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:13.120
refer to all the speakers of the Nahuatl language,&nbsp;
of whom the Mexica were just one peoples. Also to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:19.360
confuse things even more the Aztec empire didn’t&nbsp;
only have Nahuatl speakers within it and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:25.280
all Nahuatl speakers were within the Aztec Empire.&nbsp;
Therefore, if I want to talk about everyone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.800
lived in pre-columbian Central Mexico, I’ll just&nbsp;
say people in Central Mexico. I know it all sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.920
very confusing right now but pre-columbian&nbsp;
Central Mexico was anything but simple.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:42.080
So, to understand how a civilized society could&nbsp;
create an institution so prevalent with human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:47.360
sacrifices, we must first understand the&nbsp;
religion that often drove the Mexica’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:51.280
and other central Mexican people’s,&nbsp;
need for human sacrifices.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.800
Due to the often dubious nature of sources&nbsp;
we have about the Aztec Empire, its people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.200
and their religion, there are multiple&nbsp;
versions of various religious myths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.560
including the creation myth. It is also probable&nbsp;
that the people living in Central Mexico,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:12.480
who largely shared their religious beliefs with&nbsp;
the Mexica, had all slightly different versions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:18.160
of gods, myths, and rituals. Such a thing&nbsp;
is not unusual for polythiestic religions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:24.080
and we see a similar thing in ancient Greece for&nbsp;
example. With that said, there are two main Aztec&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:29.840
creation myths. I will present one of them here&nbsp;
and you can watch the other in this Ted-ed video.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:37.120
The people of central Mexico believed they lived&nbsp;
in the time of the fifth iteration of the sun;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:42.240
every previous iteration of the sun ended&nbsp;
by the destruction of the sun and the human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:48.320
race. The humans that are alive now, in the fifth&nbsp;
iteration, were created by the god Quetzalcoatl.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.120
He used the bones of the humans who lived&nbsp;
during the fourth iteration of the sun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.960
and his own blood to create the current humans.&nbsp;
The fifth sun itself was created by the god&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:04.720
Huitzilopochtli who sacrificed himself to become&nbsp;
the sun so he could nourish the world. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:10.320
Huitzilopochtli’s sister Coyolxauhqui, the&nbsp;
goddess of the moon, hated Huitzilopochtli,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:16.320
and wanted to destroy him. As such every night&nbsp;
when the moon rises, trying to destroy the sun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.320
Huitzilopochtli fights off his sister, ultimately&nbsp;
winning and creating the next day. With that said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:28.080
Huitzilopochtli needs the help of humans, through&nbsp;
the means of human sacrifices, auto-sacrifice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.880
and religious rituals, to keep successfully&nbsp;
fighting off his sister every night.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.880
Whether you choose to go with this creation&nbsp;
myth or the other one I already mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:42.320
or with the myriad of other variations on these&nbsp;
two creation myths, it doesn’t really matter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:46.000
as all of them have certain&nbsp;
central themes that don't change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.120
Same goes for the Aztec religion as a whole.&nbsp;
There might be sources with different variations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:56.480
on myths and gods but the main themes largely&nbsp;
remain the same. These main themes being;

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:02.880
The gods used their own blood to create&nbsp;
humans in one way or another and, therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:09.200
the humans are indebted by blood to the gods.
The sun needs human sacrifices so that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:14.160
is a next day. (this does not, however, mean&nbsp;
there has to be a sacrifice for each new day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:19.920
it’s not like a daily payment or something)
Human sacrifice is the most venerated way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:25.280
show proper honor to the gods and, therefore, if&nbsp;
you want something very important from them, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:31.120
for example rain for the crops from Tlaloc the god&nbsp;
of water, you have to give him a human sacrifice.&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:38.000
If there are no human sacrifices and no proper&nbsp;
honor is shown to the gods not only will the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:43.120
sun lose to the moon but also the humans&nbsp;
will be destroyed. This, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:48.800
happened in the second iteration of the sun when&nbsp;
Tezcatlipoca, the god of rulers, priests and war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.600
was disgusted by the impiety of the&nbsp;
humans and turned them all into monkeys.

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:58.880
These main factors of the Aztec religion&nbsp;
created the bases upon which the Central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:03.920
Mexican societies built their institution&nbsp;
that normalized the killing of humans.

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:09.040
Majority of people today, when someone talks&nbsp;
about the quote-unquote “Aztec sacrifices”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:13.360
picture the most common type of sacrifice,&nbsp;
and that is the cutting out of the heart and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:18.880
presenting it to the gods. However, in reality,&nbsp;
there were many different sacrificial rituals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:24.240
Some involved drowning, others burning or&nbsp;
forced starvation and some even involved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:30.160
elaborate ritualistic enacting of a predetermined&nbsp;
battle where the unarmed sacrificial victim was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:36.480
killed by several heavily armed soldiers. Plus,&nbsp;
different gods and festivals along with different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:42.400
circumstances like a drought or flooding required&nbsp;
different rituals. The victims of these rituals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:48.800
also varied, most numerous were enemies captured&nbsp;
in a war, but slaves, regular citizens and even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:54.320
sometimes noblemen were also sacrificed in&nbsp;
certain circumstances. The age and gender&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.320 --> 00:07:00.400
of the victims also mattered as, for example,&nbsp;
Tlaloc, the god of rain and water, required child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:06.160
sacrifices for the rains to come, while Toci,&nbsp;
the mother goddess, required a female sacrifice.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:11.040
Before I mention any details about the&nbsp;
quote-unquote “Aztec human sacrifices”, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:17.520
must be said is that, unless directly specified,&nbsp;
I am not only talking about the Mexica here. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:23.200
many of the sources that talk about sacrifices in&nbsp;
detail are about the Mexica but my presentation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:28.720
an “Aztec human sacrifice” is a general overview&nbsp;
of the type of sacrifice that was conducted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:35.040
as far as we know, in most of late pre-Columbian&nbsp;
central Mexico. We know from some sources that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:41.760
various Nahuas and other peoples of Central Mexico&nbsp;
did share very similar religious beliefs, customs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:47.760
and did conduct similar human sacrifices, as&nbsp;
one Nahua put it, “Before them (the gods),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:51.920
we kiss the ground, we bleed.&nbsp;
We pay our debts to the gods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:57.920
offer incense, make human sacrifices ... We&nbsp;
live by the grace of the gods.” Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:03.200
it would be reasonable to think that the details&nbsp;
we have about the Mexica human sacrifices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:08.800
are similar to that of the sacrifices conducted&nbsp;
by other Nahuas and Central Mexican peoples.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:13.600
Especially when it comes to the most common&nbsp;
type of sacrifice, the extraction of the heart.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:16.126
(fade out and in)

00:08:16.126 --> 00:08:21.360
So, imagine you’re a warrior captured in&nbsp;
a battle by the enemy who promptly brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.920
you to their city temple to sacrifice&nbsp;
you. This is not unusual. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:31.680
the society you live in has created a system&nbsp;
of ritualistic wars, called the flower wars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:37.040
one of the purposes of which was to capture enemy&nbsp;
soldiers for sacrifices. The battles of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:42.960
flower wars occurred on predetermined plains with&nbsp;
predetermined army sizes. The point of the battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:48.960
wasn’t to kill the enemy but to capture as many&nbsp;
enemies as you can so that you can sacrifice them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:55.440
in your city temple. People would still die during&nbsp;
these battles but not as many as in an actual war.

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:00.080
By the way, a bit of a side note, the&nbsp;
Mexica, and other central Mexican peoples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:06.000
could also wage normal war. There’s a common&nbsp;
misconception that when the Spanish arrived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:11.440
the Mexica lost so easily to them because they&nbsp;
tried to fight them in the style of the flower war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:16.960
and tried to capture them rather than killing&nbsp;
them, but that’s not true. The Mexica were fully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:22.720
capable of waging a quote-unquote “ordinary&nbsp;
war of conquest”. Of course, when possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:28.720
they would capture some enemies for sacrifices&nbsp;
but that wasn’t their goal. Their goal was to win,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:35.040
and as Ross Hassig states in his book Aztec&nbsp;
Warfare, “As demonstrated by the use of ambushes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.680
circumvention of battling armies, and&nbsp;
firing of enemy temples [and cities], the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:46.240
Aztec (Mexica) leadership was not overly concerned&nbsp;
with how victories were won in wars of conquest”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:52.480
If needed for victory, the Mexica were not&nbsp;
against employing the tactics of a total war.

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:57.280
Anyways, back to sacrificing you. You go&nbsp;
through a series of rituals and cleansing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:01.760
to prepare you to be sacrificed, after which&nbsp;
you’re taken to the foot of the temple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:06.400
As you’re waiting at the foot of the pyramid,&nbsp;
you’re not there alone. Depending what deity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:11.200
you’re being sacrificed for or during which&nbsp;
type of ceremony you’re being sacrificed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:15.680
there could be any number of people waiting&nbsp;
there with you. These could be other fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:21.040
warriors captured in the battle, or some slaves&nbsp;
bought from the market, or some local citizens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:25.760
that were chosen to be sacrificed along&nbsp;
with you. For now let's say there are some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:31.040
local citizens there ready to be sacrificed along&nbsp;
with you and a bunch of other captured warriors.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:35.760
Some local people walk up to their fellow&nbsp;
citizens waiting to be sacrificed and tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:41.120
them their personal stories and problems and&nbsp;
politely ask them to bring these issues up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:46.480
the gods once they’re sacrificed. The citizens&nbsp;
ready to be sacrificed listen carefully to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:53.280
fellow citizens' problems and in return are being&nbsp;
given great praise and admiration by their peers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:58.640
Being sacrificed for your city is given&nbsp;
great honor in your society and the family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:04.480
you leave behind could even be able to move&nbsp;
up social ranks thanks to your sacrifice.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:10.400
With that said, you, in your current situation,&nbsp;
are given little honor. Sure there is honor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:16.400
in being sacrificed but there is little honor in&nbsp;
being sacrificed by the enemy to the enemy's gods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:21.600
However, being sacrificed is the nature of&nbsp;
life for you. It is how it is and it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:27.200
always been this; and even though there’s&nbsp;
no honor in being sacrificed by the enemy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:33.120
there is still huge dishonor in not being&nbsp;
sacrificed when you have already been chosen and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:39.440
prepared to be sacrificed. Not being sacrificed at&nbsp;
this point is worse than being sacrificed by your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:46.000
enemy to their gods. This is why when Cortes’&nbsp;
expedition freed some sacrificial victims they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:51.280
“indignantly rejected [the] offer of&nbsp;
release and demanded to be sacrificed".

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:56.000
As one 16th century Nahua put it “it&nbsp;
was never found out, whether anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:01.920
of those that were chosen [to be sacrificed]&nbsp;
had fled, for to flee seemed a thing unworthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:08.880
of men that represented such great majesty as this&nbsp;
idol, so as not to be held as cowardly and fearful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:15.040
with perpetual infamy, not only in this land,&nbsp;
but also in his own, and so they wished first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:20.720
to die to earn eternal fame, because they&nbsp;
held [this] to be glory and a happy end.”

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:25.600
However, with that said, it is also clear&nbsp;
that not all people viewed it this way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:31.040
Dying is still dying and some people did&nbsp;
not want to die even if it brought honor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:36.640
to them and their family. That is why “when&nbsp;
some captive lost his strength, fainted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:42.320
only went continually throwing himself on the&nbsp;
ground, they (the priests) just dragged him [to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:48.080
the altar] ... But when one made an effort. .&nbsp;
.he went strong of heart, he went shouting. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:54.960
did not go downcast, he did not go spiritless;&nbsp;
he went extolling, he went exalting his city.”

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:59.920
Whether you choose to go to your sacrifice at&nbsp;
the top of the pyramid with a strong conviction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:05.600
and head held high, or by throwing yourself&nbsp;
on the ground and shouting, is up to you but,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:10.880
it does go to show that there were people&nbsp;
in pre-columbian central Mexican society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:16.960
that did not want to be sacrificed and had&nbsp;
to be dragged to the altar unwillingly.

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:22.960
Nevertheless, climbing the very steep steps of the&nbsp;
pyramid which were bloodied by the corpses of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:28.640
people sacrificed before you and whose lifeless&nbsp;
bodies were thrown down the steps next to you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:33.920
must have been a very daunting task&nbsp;
even for the most devot of people.

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:39.520
Looking down from the pyramid you see a great&nbsp;
gathering of people singing ritualistic songs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:45.120
specific to that particular sacrificial event.&nbsp;
Many of the people in the crowd also participate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:50.560
in auto-sacrifice which involves the letting&nbsp;
of blood for the gods. It must have been truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:55.920
a fervent spectacle. We also know that the&nbsp;
central mexican peoples used hallucinogenic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:59.520
drugs like shrooms and others and we’re pretty&nbsp;
sure they were somehow used in ceremonies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:05.040
mostly by the nobility and the priests, but the&nbsp;
exact extent of the use of drugs in sacrificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:11.120
ceremonies is unknown. As one historian said&nbsp;
“Despite claims that coercive chemicals were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:16.560
extensively used in Aztec culture, they are&nbsp;
very rarely mentioned in accounts of sacrificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:22.080
ceremonies” therefore, we don’t really know the&nbsp;
extent of the use of these drugs in ceremonies.

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:27.360
With that said, keep in mind as you’re trying to&nbsp;
climb the steep bloodied steps of the pyramid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:31.280
with the bodies of the sacrificial&nbsp;
victims before you falling down the steps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:34.480
and the citizens behind you&nbsp;
singing while bloodletting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:38.720
you and the priest sacrificing&nbsp;
you could also be hallucinating.

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:45.360
Once you make it to the top of the pyramid,&nbsp;
one of Cortes’ conquistadors tells us the “men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:51.200
and women who were to be sacrificed to their gods&nbsp;
were thrown on their backs and of their own accord&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:56.640
remained perfectly still”. Again this probably&nbsp;
goes back to the fact that some people went to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.640 --> 00:15:02.400
the sacrifice with their heads held high, with&nbsp;
no fear, with the belief that they are doing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:08.080
right thing. However, there most certainly were&nbsp;
others who had to be held down on the sacrificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:15.600
stone as attested to by one source which stated&nbsp;
that “The five priests of sacrifice followed them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:21.200
They were to hold down the feet, hands and&nbsp;
heads of the victims … Each prisoner they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:26.480
took to the [sacrificial] place … when they had&nbsp;
forced him to stand upon the stone which was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:32.960
figure and likeness of the sun, they threw him&nbsp;
upon his back. One took him by the right arm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:38.160
another by the left, one by his left foot,&nbsp;
another by his right, while the fifth priest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:44.160
tied his neck with a cord and held him down so&nbsp;
that he could not move.” It is clear, again, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:51.040
some people had to be held down for the sacrifice&nbsp;
as they presumably did not want to be sacrificed.

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:57.680
Once the heart was extracted by a priest with an&nbsp;
extremely sharp obsidian knife it was presented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.680 --> 00:16:04.480
to the specific idol of the god the ceremony was&nbsp;
for and, afterwards, the heart was often burned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:10.560
with the ashes being kept as an important relic.&nbsp;
The now lifeless body was then tossed down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:16.400
steep steps of the pyramid and preparations were&nbsp;
made for the next person in line to be sacrificed.

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:21.040
The bodies that piled up at the bottom of&nbsp;
the pyramid where ceremoniously butchered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:26.640
and the various parts of the body were used&nbsp;
in multiple ways, the skull was put on a skull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:31.680
rack called Tzompantli, other parts of the&nbsp;
body were given to the zoo animals to eat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:36.000
and some parts of the body, on occasion&nbsp;
when the victim was captured in battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:41.760
were given to the warrior that captured the victim&nbsp;
and he would ceremoniously eat it with his family.

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:47.600
This heart extraction sacrifice I talked about is&nbsp;
the quote “most common type of sacrifice” and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:52.560
was practiced by all the major Central Mexican&nbsp;
peoples during the period of the Aztec Empire.&nbsp;

00:16:54.320 --> 00:16:58.400
The second sacrifice I am going to be&nbsp;
talking about is, as far as we know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:01.600
specific to Tenochtitlan and the Mexica people.

00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:05.760
The preparation for this sacrificial&nbsp;
ceremony started a year before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:11.680
actual ceremony in the month of Tōxcatl, which&nbsp;
is roughly the month of May. During this month&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:17.680
a young male was chosen to be the impersonator&nbsp;
of Tezcatlipoca, the god of rulers, priests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:23.600
war and a bunch of other things. This chosen&nbsp;
young man was either a slave or a war captive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:29.360
Once chosen to be the impersonator, the man was&nbsp;
taught various skills like how to play the flute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:35.840
sing, and speak in the manner befitting of god. He&nbsp;
was also painted completely black barring his eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:41.760
and adorned with precious jewellery and cotton&nbsp;
embroidered clothes. Then, for a year, the young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:47.520
man would walk around the city of Tenochtitlan,&nbsp;
playing the flute, smoking tobacco and interacting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:54.160
with the people as the earthly manifestation of&nbsp;
the god Tezcatlipoca. It is said that no one could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.160 --> 00:18:00.080
refuse the request of the impersonator and several&nbsp;
times he would even meet with the Mexica ruler.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.080 --> 00:18:06.560
The young man was also ritually wed to four&nbsp;
maidens with whom he spent a lot of time.

00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:11.040
When the year passed, on the same day&nbsp;
in May that he was chosen to be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:16.000
impersonator of Tezcatlipoca, the young&nbsp;
man walked up the steps of the pyramid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:23.040
broke his flute in half, and made preparations to&nbsp;
be sacrificed. As always a great crowd of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:28.960
would gather in front of the temple, sing songs&nbsp;
and participate in auto-sacrifice. The priests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:34.640
could only sacrifice the impersonator once he gave&nbsp;
them the approval, this way the impersonator of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:42.000
Tezcatlipoca chose his own time when to die. Once&nbsp;
dead, the body of the impersonator was flayed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:48.320
the flesh was distributed among the nobles of the&nbsp;
city and eaten; his skull, as always, was put on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:54.160
Tzompantli. Also during the ceremony other people&nbsp;
and other gods’ impersonators were sacrificed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.160 --> 00:19:00.160
and the next year’s impersonator of Tezcatlipoca&nbsp;
was chosen; and so the cycle continued.

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:06.160
As stated before, there were many&nbsp;
other types of sacrificial ceremonies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:10.000
than just the two I talked about,&nbsp;
but hopefully this closer look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:14.560
these two sacrifices gave you an idea&nbsp;
of how these ceremonies looked like.

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:20.240
Now that we have discussed why the sacrifices&nbsp;
happened, and how the sacrifices happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:25.600
let's talk about why the sacrifices happened …&nbsp;
Yes you heard right, so far we only mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:31.200
that the “Aztec religion created the bases upon&nbsp;
which the Central Mexican societies built their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:36.560
institution that normalized the killing of&nbsp;
humans” but religion wasn’t the only factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:42.320
for why it happened, it was undoubtedly a major&nbsp;
aspect that created the tools, the means, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:48.720
the bases for sacrifices to occur but according&nbsp;
to some historians it wasn’t the only reason.

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:52.880
Historians have come up with 3&nbsp;
ideas for why sacrifices were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:56.640
so prevalent in Central Mexico.&nbsp;
One of which we already discussed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.640 --> 00:20:01.120
religion, but there could have also been&nbsp;
a political and ecological aspect to it.

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:05.840
First, the Ecological argument. This&nbsp;
argument focuses on the cannibalism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:10.240
aspect of human sacrifices and was&nbsp;
proposed by historian Michael Harner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:15.920
and later revised by Marvin Harris. The main&nbsp;
core of the argument claims that cannibalism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:22.480
was a dietary necessity in Central Mexico since&nbsp;
the area lacked any kind of domesticable large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.480 --> 00:20:27.920
herbivores. This meant that pre-columbian&nbsp;
Central Mexican society was deficient in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:33.200
essential amino acids and the only way to&nbsp;
get them was through cannibalism. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:39.200
why many of the sacrificial rituals we talked&nbsp;
about involve some kind of cannibalism and why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.200 --> 00:20:45.120
the religious institution that normalized the&nbsp;
killing of humans was retained in the society.

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:49.680
This theory came under a lot of attack by&nbsp;
other historians who argued that there were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:56.240
plenty of huntable animals in central Mexico&nbsp;
like salamanders, fowls, armadillos, weasels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.240 --> 00:21:01.920
etc. and there are also accounts of the central&nbsp;
Mexican people raising large numbers of dogs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:07.440
sell and eat. Plus nutrients needed from&nbsp;
meat could also be found in local plants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:12.880
like the leaves and seeds of amaranth. There&nbsp;
is also a case to be made that the cannibalism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:17.840
aspect of Central Mexican sacrifices we&nbsp;
find in the sources could be overblown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:22.560
due to the nature of the sources but more on&nbsp;
the nature of the sources later in the video.

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:26.640
Second we’ll look at the Political&nbsp;
argument. The political argument was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:31.760
championed by historians Godfrey Conrad&nbsp;
and Arthur Demarest. This argument,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:36.640
rather than trying to explain the practice of&nbsp;
human sacrifices in central Mexico as a whole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:41.760
focuses more on the rulers of the Aztec&nbsp;
Empire, the Mexica. This is because even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:46.880
though, as discussed, human sacrifices were&nbsp;
prevalent in all central Mexican societies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:53.360
it can be argued that the Mexica brought the scale&nbsp;
of human sacrifice to an extent previously unseen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:58.720
in central Mexico. From the sources we have,&nbsp;
it seems like the Mexica sacrificed way more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.720 --> 00:22:03.600
people than any other central Mexican society&nbsp;
did and they may have even used these extensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:08.880
sacrifices as a scare tactic against their&nbsp;
enemies and to keep their vassals in check.

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:13.280
The political explanation argues that the&nbsp;
Mexica, when they became the hegemon of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:18.960
the triple alliance that formed the Aztec Empire,&nbsp;
created an imperial cult around their patron god,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:24.720
Huitzilopochtli, that imposed on the Mexica&nbsp;
the religious duty of warfare, conquest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:30.960
and sacrifices. In essence, the religion of the&nbsp;
Mexica was manipulated for political reasons to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:36.640
create the basis upon which the policy of&nbsp;
imperialism could be conducted more freely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.640 --> 00:22:41.040
and efficiently. The religion that&nbsp;
already practiced human sacrifices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:47.040
but not to such an extent, was altered to fit&nbsp;
the political agenda of expansion and conquest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:53.360
Through the religious demand for more sacrifices&nbsp;
the Mexica could easily conduct and justify their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.360 --> 00:22:59.280
frequent wars and subjugations which on top of&nbsp;
expending their economic and political power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:03.920
also gave them a steady supply of&nbsp;
the much needed sacrificial victims.

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:08.000
The political argument has also come under&nbsp;
attack from some historians who believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:14.400
it overshadows the pure religious importance of&nbsp;
human sacrifices in the Mexica society. Herbert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:22.080
Burhenn states that “We are asked to accept the&nbsp;
intensification of human sacrifice and cannibalism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:28.880
as rational because it led to Mexica prosperity&nbsp;
and hegemony. The Mexica leaders were not blinded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.880 --> 00:23:34.880
by superstition into ordering vicious and brutal&nbsp;
acts but were rather pursuing eminently rational,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.880 --> 00:23:40.560
if somewhat short-sighted, dictates of Realpolitik&nbsp;
… We may be able to recognize the rational shape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:46.240
of the actions of Mexica leaders, but we do not&nbsp;
know whether they considered their actions in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:51.840
terms of the reasons we infer.” They may just have&nbsp;
been acting the way they did because of increasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.840 --> 00:23:57.760
religious fanaticism which was correlated with the&nbsp;
expansion of the Empire but did not dictate it.

00:23:58.320 --> 00:24:03.360
I am not convinced by the Ecological argument.&nbsp;
I also believe religion had a very strong role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.360 --> 00:24:09.120
to play in why, essentially, the killing of humans&nbsp;
in a ritualistic manner was so normalized in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.120 --> 00:24:14.960
societies of central Mexico. However, the Aztec&nbsp;
Empire and Central Mexico as a whole was quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.960 --> 00:24:19.600
a diverse place and the reasons for human&nbsp;
sacrifices by one peoples could have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:24.240
different from other peoples. All of them&nbsp;
definitely had a religious reason but when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:30.240
it comes to the powerful states such as that of&nbsp;
the Mexica I don’t think religion was the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:35.920
factor that drove the sacrificial rituals. There&nbsp;
must have been some kind of political involvement.

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:40.560
Lastly, I also don’t think the sacrificing&nbsp;
of humans was as normalized in central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.560 --> 00:24:45.360
Mexican society as much as we may think&nbsp;
it was. On top of the sources mentioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:49.600
that being sacrificed was an honor,&nbsp;
something a person would want to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.600 --> 00:24:54.160
there are also snipits showing that some&nbsp;
people did not want to be sacrificed as I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.160 --> 00:24:58.640
have demonstrated when talking about the&nbsp;
specific rituals. There are also hints in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.640 --> 00:25:04.640
the mythology that not everyone in the Central&nbsp;
Mexican society were happy with the sacrifices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:11.520
for example, the god Quetzalcoatl is mentioned in&nbsp;
multiple myths as being against human sacrifices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:18.320
to quote “it is told and related that many times&nbsp;
during the life of Quetzalcoatl, sorceres tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.320 --> 00:25:23.840
to ridicule him into making the human payment,&nbsp;
into taking human lifes [as payment/tribute].&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:25.040 --> 00:25:30.560
But he always refused. He did not consent,&nbsp;
because he greatly loved his subjects.”

00:25:30.560 --> 00:25:35.280
There were also many people in the society&nbsp;
who probably never thought much of the human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:41.520
sacrifices and or, even though against it, went&nbsp;
with it because that was the nature of life for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:41.520 --> 00:25:47.440
them. As historian Camilla Townsend put it “Surely&nbsp;
there were many more of the Mexica who simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:47.440 --> 00:25:54.240
never thought much about it—like people in so many&nbsp;
times and places who choose not to see the pain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.240 --> 00:26:00.000
inflicted on other people when it is more&nbsp;
convenient not to.” It would, therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:05.440
be inappropriate to talk about all the Central&nbsp;
Mexican peoples or even all the Mexica as being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:05.440 --> 00:26:12.320
completely fine with human sacrifices but, at the&nbsp;
same time, it is definitive that human sacrifices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.320 --> 00:26:18.480
were normalized to a large extent and seen as a&nbsp;
fact of life for the majority of the population.

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:28.240
Lastly, now we come to the problem with everything&nbsp;
I just talked about. Basically all we know about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:28.240 --> 00:26:34.320
the people in the Aztec Empire and the surrounding&nbsp;
area comes from mostly colonial era sources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:38.720
These sources include accounts of&nbsp;
conquistadors, general colonizers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:43.520
Nahua songs, and books written primarily by&nbsp;
Nahuas or Nahuatl-speaking peoples, but not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:48.960
only them, which are called codices. The Nahuas&nbsp;
“wrote” codices before the Spanish arrival but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.960 --> 00:26:54.560
the majority of them did not survive as they were&nbsp;
extensively burned by the Spanish and the Catholic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:59.541
church. Today there are only three complete&nbsp;
pre-colonial Mexica codices (Codex Borbonicus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.541 --> 00:27:01.520
the Matrícula de Tributos and the Codex&nbsp;
Boturini), and even these three codices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.520 --> 00:27:06.880
are being questioned by historians whether they&nbsp;
truly were made in the pre-colonial era as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:13.360
do have some similarities to colonial codices.&nbsp;
Nevertheless, since we have only three surviving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:19.760
possibly pre-colonial Mexica codices, today’s&nbsp;
historians have to rely a lot on colonial sources.

00:27:19.760 --> 00:27:24.240
You probably know that relying on&nbsp;
colonial sources isn’t the best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:30.400
as accounts of conquistadors and colonizers were&nbsp;
often biased against the natives to justify their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:30.400 --> 00:27:35.760
conquest. For example, Cortes’ first letter&nbsp;
to the Spanish king Charles the Fifth was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:35.760 --> 00:27:41.200
found to be heavily edited by a royal committee&nbsp;
before it got to the king. The committee wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:41.200 --> 00:27:47.360
to present Cortes’ actions in Mexico the most&nbsp;
favourably for the Spanish Crown and, therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.360 --> 00:27:52.960
they altered parts of it before showing it&nbsp;
to the king. This existent bias is why I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:52.960 --> 00:27:59.040
mentioned the cannibalism in our sources could be&nbsp;
overplayed. It is also due to this Spanish bias&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:03.760
why a lot of the numbers about how many&nbsp;
people were being sacrificed by the Mexica,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:09.600
or central Mexican peoples in general, are&nbsp;
sometimes laughably over exaggerated. To give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:15.520
an example. When the main pyramid of Tenochtitlan,&nbsp;
known today as Templo Mayor, was refurbished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.520 --> 00:28:22.640
and reworked in 1487, a giant 4 day dedication&nbsp;
ceremony was held at the top of the temple with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:22.640 --> 00:28:28.800
4 separate sacrificial altars. Some of the Spanish&nbsp;
colonial sources state that during this ceremony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:36.880
80,400 people were sacrificed. Which is roughly&nbsp;
14 people a minute or 3.5 people per minute per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:43.360
alter for 96 hours straight. This is obviously&nbsp;
logistically impossible and such a high number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.360 --> 00:28:48.320
is certainly a Spanish fabrication. Most&nbsp;
historians today put the number of sacrifices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.320 --> 00:28:53.840
for the dedication of the temple somewhere&nbsp;
between 4 to 20 thousand, which must be stated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:53.840 --> 00:29:00.320
even at its lower end, is still a very sizable&nbsp;
number for just a single event. According to one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:00.320 --> 00:29:06.720
source the smell of blood was so strong in the&nbsp;
city that it became “unendurable to the people”.

00:29:06.720 --> 00:29:12.160
Over all you can clearly see the problematic&nbsp;
nature of using colonial sources. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:12.160 --> 00:29:17.840
as always, there’s much more nuance to this&nbsp;
statement. For example, as already mentioned there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:17.840 --> 00:29:23.920
are colonial codices that were written by Nahuas.&nbsp;
This would in theory be great as we get their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.920 --> 00:29:29.120
opinion but there’s more caveats to that. For&nbsp;
example, as mentioned at the start of the video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.120 --> 00:29:36.560
not all Nahuas were the Mexica or were within the&nbsp;
Aztec Empire. Also not all Nahuas liked the Mexica&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:36.560 --> 00:29:42.080
or other Nahuas, and it is sometimes hard in the&nbsp;
colonial period to separate the various Nahuas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:42.080 --> 00:29:47.520
from each other, as the Spanish, and sometimes&nbsp;
even the various Nahuas themselves, just called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:54.080
everyone Nahuas or, even more broadly, Indians&nbsp;
or Amerindians. It is also due to this Spanish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.080 --> 00:29:59.440
treatment of grouping all the Amerindians together&nbsp;
that the societal and cultural differences that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:59.440 --> 00:30:05.120
existed in the pre-colonial period, among various&nbsp;
Nahuas and also other Central Mexican peoples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:05.120 --> 00:30:11.040
got muddied and often coalesced in the colonial&nbsp;
era. As historian Ramos Gabriela put it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:11.040 --> 00:30:16.480
“An important change that marked the colonial&nbsp;
period was that indigenous intellectuals had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:16.480 --> 00:30:22.560
to negotiate the Spanish idea of the colonial&nbsp;
Indian”. Because the Spanish just put them all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.560 --> 00:30:27.280
together. All of this makes the interpretation&nbsp;
of the colonial sources even harder as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:32.000
can represent a collage of traditions&nbsp;
from various pre-Columbian peoples. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:37.600
there were codices that did talk about the history&nbsp;
of other peoples or tribes than just the Mexica.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.600 --> 00:30:42.480
For example, Codex Xolotl that, even though,&nbsp;
talked about the history of the entire valley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:42.480 --> 00:30:47.200
of Mexico, focused more on the people of&nbsp;
Texcoco rather than the Mexica. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:47.200 --> 00:30:52.480
just a few generations after the conquest&nbsp;
the tradition of separate peoples or tribes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:52.480 --> 00:30:58.560
living in central Mexico mostly died out with&nbsp;
larger cultural and ethnic groupings forming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:58.560 --> 00:31:04.000
like that of the Nahuas or again, even&nbsp;
more broadly, the Indians/Amerindians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:09.440
and the colonial written histories, even though&nbsp;
some more than others, reflected this change.

00:31:09.440 --> 00:31:14.160
On top of dealing with this cultural and ethnic&nbsp;
nuance of the sources, we also have to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.160 --> 00:31:17.027
the fact that many codices like The Florentine&nbsp;
Codex (probably the most important ethnographic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.027 --> 00:31:20.160
research study on the Mexica written in the 16th&nbsp;
century) were written by native Nahuatl speakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:20.160 --> 00:31:25.840
but under the supervision of Spanish friars. In&nbsp;
the case of the Florentine Codex it was Bernardino&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.840 --> 00:31:31.120
de Sahagún. Therefore, whether the Florentine&nbsp;
codex represents the genuent information of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.120 --> 00:31:37.280
native Nahua population or a heavily edited&nbsp;
version of it by a Spanish friar is unknown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.280 --> 00:31:42.720
and many other codices like Codex Mendoza were&nbsp;
written in a similar context. This colonial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:50.240
context should, however, not always be considered&nbsp;
bad. For example, a Dominican friar, Diego Durán,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:50.240 --> 00:31:55.760
who grew up in Mexico as his family moved&nbsp;
to the colony very early after the conquest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:55.760 --> 00:32:00.880
knew Nahuatl as he grew up there. He was&nbsp;
also friends with many local Nahuas and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:06.720
interviewed many elder Nahuatl speakers for his&nbsp;
book The History of the Indies of New Spain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:11.920
During his time he was even criticized&nbsp;
for being too favorable to the natives.

00:32:11.920 --> 00:32:17.200
So as always there’s a lot of problems with&nbsp;
the sources but there’s also some good in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:17.200 --> 00:32:22.560
them. Which makes the whole thing of relying&nbsp;
on them that much harder because we cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:22.560 --> 00:32:27.760
simply disregard them but also have to be&nbsp;
very careful with what we take from them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.760 --> 00:32:32.240
This is for example the reason why I did&nbsp;
not talk about any specific numbers of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:32.240 --> 00:32:38.080
many sacrifices were conducted per year by the&nbsp;
Mexica or the whole Valley of Mexico in general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:38.080 --> 00:32:43.120
because with the nature of the sources it is&nbsp;
really hard to tell. There definitely were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:47.840
human sacrifices and cannibalism happening&nbsp;
in pre-columbian central Mexico, the sheer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:52.080
amount of written and archaeological&nbsp;
evidence for that is unquestionable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.080 --> 00:32:58.080
but extrapolating numbers of victims being&nbsp;
sacrificed every year from that is really hard.

00:32:59.200 --> 00:33:04.240
Lastly, it is prudent to mention that the&nbsp;
Spanish weren’t the only ones that altered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.240 --> 00:33:10.400
sources we rely on today, the Mexica did the&nbsp;
same thing. King Itzcoatl who ruled over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:10.400 --> 00:33:17.440
Mexica from 1427–1440 destroyed most of the&nbsp;
early historical codices of the Mexica people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:17.440 --> 00:33:22.720
and of their conquered subjects, because he&nbsp;
said it is “not wise that all the people should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:22.720 --> 00:33:28.160
know the histories.” Itzcoatl did this right&nbsp;
after he came to the throne of Tenochtitlan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:28.160 --> 00:33:31.840
and secured the independence of the&nbsp;
Mexica people from their enemies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:31.840 --> 00:33:38.000
therefore, his action of burning books can be&nbsp;
interpreted as a way of solidifying a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:38.000 --> 00:33:43.040
national historical narrative which might&nbsp;
not have been completely true. It is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:43.040 --> 00:33:47.440
this event that proponents of the Political&nbsp;
argument point to when they talk about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:47.440 --> 00:33:53.360
creation of an imperial cult that drove the large&nbsp;
number of sacrifices conducted by the Mexica.

00:33:53.360 --> 00:33:59.280
So to summarize. The history of the Mexica people,&nbsp;
or the Aztec Empire in general, that we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:05.520
today, and that is presented in books and channels&nbsp;
like mine or Invicta, is a history that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:11.600
altered by King Itzcoatl who was trying to build a&nbsp;
national narrative, whose history largely survives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:11.600 --> 00:34:17.360
in colonial Spanish sources that were written with&nbsp;
their own biases but all of that does not mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:17.360 --> 00:34:23.680
that all the sources are unusable. There are many&nbsp;
facts and themes shared among all the sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:23.680 --> 00:34:29.760
there are also genuent Nahua statements in many&nbsp;
colonial codices, and by cross referencing all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:29.760 --> 00:34:35.120
this and using the historical method we can reach&nbsp;
a very well informed understanding of not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:35.120 --> 00:34:41.760
the Mexica, or Nahua societies but of all the&nbsp;
pre-columbian Central Mexican societies. Through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:47.120
this we can gain a much better picture of how&nbsp;
these societies functioned and how they came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.120 --> 00:34:54.000
normalize, even though not fully, and ritualize,&nbsp;
the act of killing humans in a religious context.

00:34:57.120 --> 00:35:01.920
Before I say anything, go check out Stefan Milo’s&nbsp;
